include includes/folio-shared.pug

html(lang="en")
    head
        +folioHead('Design Dash: tracing a screen back to a decision | Aaron Benjamin', 'An open-source product-design workflow that connects evidence, objects, requirements, and interface decisions.', '/design-dash.html', 'folio', '/assets/img/folio/project--design-dash/design-dash--work-card-2x.png')
    body.folio.folio--case
        +folioShell
        +folioHeader('work', 'Design Dash', '/#design-dash')
        main#main.folio-main.folio-main--case
            article.folio-case
                header.folio-case__hero
                    .folio-case__hero-media
                        +storyFlow({id: "dash-hero-caption", title: "Design Dash · From a product question to a buildable plan", nodes: [{label: "Evidence", detail: "What do we know, and what are we assuming?"}, {label: "System", detail: "Which objects, actions, and rules make up the product?"}, {label: "Decision", detail: "Which approach is worth building, and why?"}, {label: "Plan", detail: "Requirements, wireframes, and a way to learn."}], caption: "Each interface decision must connect to the product reasoning behind it."})
                    .folio-case__intro
                        p.folio-eyebrow Open-source method
                        h1 The screen was ready before the team understood it.
                        p.folio-case__dek I built Design Dash to connect product reasoning to interface decisions. Generating a screen is fast. Knowing what it should do takes more work.
                    +caseFacts
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Role
                            dd Author and facilitator
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Scope
                            dd Nine-phase workflow for chats, agents, and workshops
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Key decision
                            dd One file-based model, with traceable decisions and generated views
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Evidence
                            dd Used in Renaissance assignment workshops; public method and accepted architecture

                +caseSection({labelledby: "dash-files-title", prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow I — Inspiration
                        h2#dash-files-title The files existed. The reasoning was hard to follow.
                        p A prototype can look coherent while the decisions behind it remain scattered across notes and conversations. I built Design Dash around a question the interface alone could not answer: how would a team trace this screen back to the problem and evidence that justified it?
                        p A workshop could produce assumptions, a specification, a flow, a wireframe, and a pitch. Their relationships lived in the prose. To understand why a control existed, someone had to search the folder.
                        p AI made the gap more obvious. A prompt could return cards and filters before the team had named the product’s objects or separated evidence from assumptions. I wanted the workflow to preserve those decisions, not just its outputs.
                        p On the Renaissance Assignments page, I facilitated product and engineering workshops around a shared assignment model. Title, type, assigned date, and due date were common to every assignment. Pages read and questions answered remained product-specific. That distinction gave us a structure we could prototype and test.
                    +storyComparison({id: "dash-trace-caption", title: "From separate deliverables to connected decisions", columns: [{label: "The gap", title: "A folder of outputs", items: ["Assumptions, specifications, and wireframes", "Relationships explained only in prose", "A screen can lose its rationale"]}, {label: "The direction", title: "An evidence trail", items: ["Opportunity → requirement → decision", "Objects define what the interface acts on", "A screen points back to a decision"]}], caption: "The method makes the relationships between artifacts explicit."})

                +imageBrief({"role": "Context", "title": "Outputs scattered across a folder", "composition": "Arrange evidence, assumptions, requirements, and a screen as disconnected documents; ask which decision links them.", "purpose": "Establish the opening tension.", "caption": "Illustrative reconstruction of the traceability problem.", "ratio": "16:9", "type": "Labeled reconstruction"})
                +caseSection({labelledby: "dash-rigor-title", tint: true, prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow D — Design Process
                        h2#dash-rigor-title Some decisions needed more review than others
                        p I made the review tier depend on risk, reversibility, and reach. A narrow, reversible change should not require the same process as a regulated workflow. Every tier still has an ethics floor.
                        p Standard work requires evidence, comparison of concepts, reconciliation with the user’s mental model, ethics review, and a learning plan. High-stakes work cannot waive those gates. Simulated reviewers do not count as specialist sign-off.
                    +storyComparison({id: "dash-tiers-caption", title: "Three review tiers", columns: [{label: "Express", title: "Narrow and reversible", detail: "Keep an ethics floor. Record skipped gates as evidence debt."}, {label: "Standard", title: "Evidence and comparison", detail: "Require evidence, model reconciliation, selection, ethics, and learning."}, {label: "High-stakes", title: "No gate waivers", detail: "Use the full review path for broad, regulated, or hard-to-undo work."}], caption: "The tier changes the depth of review, not the obligation to record the decision."})


                +imageBrief({"role": "Process", "title": "The decision and its alternatives", "composition": "Connect opportunity → requirement → decision → screen; show the Dash Model feeding a current plan and release snapshot.", "purpose": "Make the research-to-decision connection visible.", "caption": "A reconstructed comparison of the choices described in this story.", "ratio": "3:2", "type": "Labeled reconstruction"})
                +caseSection({labelledby: "dash-engagement"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow E — Engagement
                    h2#dash-engagement Make the model usable in a working session
                    p The Renaissance assignment workshops gave the approach a concrete use. I facilitated sessions with product and engineering around the shared assignment model, while UX research tested the resulting prototypes. People could discuss a product object, see its interface implications, and record unresolved questions in the same working session.
                    p The method had to support that conversation without making a rich document or a particular toolchain compulsory. Keeping a linked file model as the source lets chats and workshops contribute to the same record. The living plan is a generated view, rather than another document everyone has to keep synchronized.
                +caseSection({labelledby: "dash-store-title", prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow A — Action
                        h2#dash-store-title A rich document couldn’t be the only way to use the method
                        p An early approach treated a rich living-plan document as the source of truth when a local toolchain was available. That excluded people working in a chat or worksheet and created another summary that could drift.
                        p I accepted a file-based architecture: Markdown records with small headers and typed links form the Dash Model. Chat and workshops write to those records. The Design Plan regenerates from them; a Story freezes a release snapshot. Neither view becomes a second editable source.
                        p I rejected a database as the canonical store because the method needs to work without one. The tradeoff is that the files need explicit validation. An interface must link to a decision, the decision to a requirement or object, and the requirement to an opportunity.
                    +storyFlow({id: "dash-model-caption", title: "One source, two kinds of output", nodes: [{label: "Chat or workshop", detail: "Capture evidence, assumptions, and decisions."}, {label: "Dash Model", detail: "Edit the linked, plain-file records."}, {label: "Design Plan", detail: "Regenerate the current working view."}, {label: "Story", detail: "Freeze a release snapshot."}], caption: "The plan and story derive from the model; they are not competing sources of truth."})

                +caseOutcome("dash-outcome-title", "The assignment workshops put the method into practice")
                    p The Renaissance workshops produced object notes, diagrams, wireframes, and a low-fidelity prototype the team tested. The shipped assignment view came from that shared model.
                    p The workflow is public. The file-based Dash Model is an accepted architecture, with existing dashes moving onto it incrementally. I have no public usage count. The useful test is whether a team can inspect a design decision and find the evidence and product rules behind it.
                +imageBrief({"role": "Outcome", "title": "What the evidence supports", "composition": "Use only the results stated in this case. Keep population, timeframe, qualitative status, and any attribution limits beside the result.", "purpose": "Resolve the story without overstating impact.", "caption": "Evidence summary reconstructed from the documented account; no invented measurements.", "ratio": "16:9", "type": "Evidence summary"})
            +folioFooter(false, 'I’m happy to walk through how the method fits a real product problem.', {href: '/many-hats.html', label: 'Next: Many Hats'})
